# DeskCrab configuration
# Copy to ~/.config/deskcrab/deskcrab.conf and edit.

# Required: path to piper-tts voice model (.onnx)
PIPER_VOICE="$HOME/.local/share/piper/en_US-lessac-medium.onnx"

# Optional: piper length scale — controls speech speed.
# 1.0 = default. Lower = faster (e.g. 0.9 ≈ 10% faster). Higher = slower.
# PIPER_LENGTH_SCALE="0.9"

# Optional: piper speaker index — only used for multi-speaker models like libritts.
# Integer index (0-based) into the model's speaker map. Single-speaker models ignore this.
# PIPER_SPEAKER="79"

# Required: path to whisper.cpp model
WHISPER_MODEL="/usr/share/whisper.cpp-model-small.en/ggml-small.en.bin"

# Optional: model and effort level. The engine follows the model name
# (specs/model-backends.md): a Claude name runs the Claude CLI account walk
# below; an OpenAI name — "sol" (an alias for CODEX_MODEL_SOL), any "gpt-*"
# slug, or a "codex:<slug>" spelling — runs the Codex CLI on the machine's
# logged-in ChatGPT subscription. Efforts are the same words on both engines
# (low|medium|high|xhigh|max), plus "ultra" on codex only. Every model knob in
# this file routes the same way: WAKE_MODEL, JOB_MODEL, DISPUTE_MODEL, the
# chess movers, the classifiers.
# CLAUDE_MODEL="opus"
# CLAUDE_EFFORT="low"

# Optional: the Codex engine. Auth comes from CODEX_HOME (default ~/.codex) —
# log in once with `codex login` (ChatGPT plan; an OPENAI_API_KEY in the
# environment is deliberately ignored). Codex has one login, so a limit never
# walks accounts: it benches the engine for CODEX_LIMIT_COOLDOWN seconds and
# the turn or wake falls back to the Claude walk at CODEX_FALLBACK_MODEL; a
# builder job is blocked and retried, never downgraded.
# CODEX_BIN="$HOME/.local/bin/codex"
# CODEX_MODEL_SOL="gpt-5.6-sol"    # what the "sol" alias resolves to
# CODEX_FALLBACK_MODEL="opus"      # default: CLAUDE_MODEL
# CODEX_LIMIT_COOLDOWN=1800
# CODEX_PROMPT_MODE="instructions" # "preface" keeps codex's own base
                                   # instructions and rides the prompt on the
                                   # user text instead

# Optional: further Claude Code config directories (separate `claude` logins /
# subscriptions) to fall back to when the account in hand refuses to run at all
# — session/usage limit reached or credits exhausted. The refused run is
# retried with CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR pointed at each in turn. Applies everywhere the
# assistant runs claude: interactive turns, autonomous wakes, detached jobs,
# and conversation summarization. Limit refusals only — auth and network
# failures still surface as themselves.
#
# One entry or many: whitespace- or colon-separated, tried left to right. Set up
# each login once with its own /login (never copy credentials between config
# dirs — racing token refreshes corrupt them):
#   CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=$HOME/.claude-fallback claude /login
# CLAUDE_FALLBACK_CONFIG_DIR="$HOME/.claude-fallback"
# CLAUDE_FALLBACK_CONFIG_DIR="$HOME/.claude-fallback:$HOME/.claude-fallback2"

# The accounts form one flat numbered list, all equal: account 1 is
# $HOME/.claude, accounts 2..N the entries above in configured order. When an
# account refuses over a limit, it cools down (hours, sized to the refusal's
# own wording) and the next account not in cooldown becomes the current —
# durably, wrapping past the end of the list — and stays current until it
# refuses in its turn. Nothing switches back on a timer. The current and the
# per-account cooldowns live in one small state file,
# ~/.local/share/deskcrab/account-state; delete it and account 1 answers with
# nothing cooling (`crab status` leads with which account answers next and
# why the state last moved).

# Optional: project directory (Claude runs from here, gets project memory)
# PROJECT_DIR="$HOME"

# Optional: conversation archive directory
# ARCHIVE_DIR="$HOME/.local/share/deskcrab/archive"

# Optional: conversation timeout in seconds before archiving (default: 300 = 5 min)
# CONVO_TIMEOUT=300

# Optional: sliding-window history to keep long conversations cheap.
# Once the live convo exceeds CONVO_MAX_TURNS blocks, the oldest
# CONVO_SUMMARIZE_TURNS blocks are folded into a running summary (kept above the
# recent turns) using CONVO_SUMMARY_MODEL. Recent turns stay verbatim.
# A block is one thing said by one side — a "User: " line or an "Assistant: "
# line — so an autonomous wake, which has no user line above it, counts too.
# CONVO_MAX_TURNS=20
# CONVO_SUMMARIZE_TURNS=10
# CONVO_SUMMARY_MODEL="haiku"

# Optional: custom system prompt file — appended to the core prompt
# Use this for project-specific context, custom instructions, etc.
# CUSTOM_PROMPT="$HOME/.config/deskcrab/custom-prompt.md"

# Optional: whisper transcription fixes (sed expressions applied to transcribed text)
# Useful for proper nouns that whisper consistently gets wrong.
# Example: WHISPER_FIXES='s/mycool app/MyCoolApp/gi; s/\bhy plant\b/Hyprland/gi'
# WHISPER_FIXES=''

# Optional: TTS pronunciation fixes (sed expressions applied to spoken text)
# Useful for words piper mispronounces or spells out letter-by-letter.
# Applied only to the spoken reply — display-channel content is untouched.
# Example: TTS_FIXES='s/\bhmph\b/humph/gi'
# TTS_FIXES=''

# What the CLI puts in front of her, per run. Measured numbers and the
# reasoning are in tools/context-probe-results.md; the defaults are the point
# of that measurement, so change them knowing what each one costs.
#
# Every built-in tool is worth roughly 1,900 tokens on every session, so this
# list is the biggest single lever in the whole prompt. ToolSearch and Agent
# are deliberately absent: with no MCP servers there is nothing for ToolSearch
# to search, and a subagent holds the turn open while the user waits.
# CLAUDE_TOOLS_TURN="Bash,Read,Write,Edit,WebFetch,WebSearch"
# CLAUDE_TOOLS_WAKE="$CLAUDE_TOOLS_TURN"
# CLAUDE_TOOLS_JOB=""          # empty = no restriction; a builder needs its tools
#
# The skills catalogue is 6,690 tokens on every session and she used one skill.
# Set to 1 to buy it back.
# CLAUDE_SKILLS=0

# Prompt layer budgets, in bytes, PROMPT_BUDGET_<layer>_<profile>. Defaults are
# the table in specs/prompt-assembly.md §11. `crab context --layers` prints
# what each layer actually measured and whether it fitted.
#
# The one to know about: L1 is identity plus the persona sheet, and its budget
# is 4,000. A persona sheet larger than that has whole markdown sections left
# off the end — the prompt names them and says where they are, but they are not
# in front of her. Either slim the sheet against the identity layer and conduct
# (it is meant to hold what those two do NOT say), or raise the budget here.
# PROMPT_BUDGET_L1_TURN=4000
# PROMPT_BUDGET_L6_TURN=8000

# CONTEXT_FILES is gone. It appended whole files to the assistant's prompt, and
# the example it shipped with was a project's CLAUDE.md — instructions written
# for a coding agent, in a different voice, addressed to a different reader,
# arriving on every turn including one-word answers. Nothing that belongs to
# the desktop coding agent may enter her sessions (specs/prompt-assembly.md
# §16), and a knob whose documented use was exactly that had to go rather than
# be left as a loaded gun in the example config. A file she should be able to
# reach is named in the WHERE THINGS ARE index instead, which costs one line
# and lets her open it when the turn needs it.

# Optional: durable "wants" file — the assistant's own long-term goals, which it
# maintains itself and pursues during autonomous wakes (crab wake / crab wake-at,
# or the systemd/deskcrab-wake.timer template). Unset = feature off.
# WANTS_FILE="$HOME/.local/share/deskcrab/wants.md"

# Optional: quiet hours (local time, HH-HH, wraps midnight) — autonomous wakes
# still run and can work silently, but never speak or open windows during these hours.
# WAKE_QUIET_HOURS="23-09"

# Optional: effort level for autonomous wakes (default: CLAUDE_EFFORT).
# Nobody is waiting on a wake, so it can afford more thinking.
# WAKE_EFFORT="medium"

# Optional: the staleness gate on held wake notes (specs/wake-queue.md rule
# 27b). A note held for a hot conversation comes back through the queue; before
# it is delivered, a cheap bounded judge reads what has actually been said
# since the note was written and drops it silently when the conversation has
# already covered or overtaken it — a held note must not announce as fresh news
# a thing the user was told half an hour ago. Only a positive verdict drops;
# a judge that fails or times out delivers the note.
# WAKE_STALE_GATE=1                 # set 0 to deliver held notes unjudged
# WAKE_STALE_MODEL="claude-fable-5" # the staleness judge
# WAKE_STALE_TIMEOUT=45             # seconds before the judge is abandoned

# Optional: seconds without life signs before an autonomous wake is presumed
# hung and reaped (default: 300). There is no wall-clock limit on wakes — a
# session counts as alive while it streams output OR its process tree burns
# CPU (long compiles count as work). Raise it if your wakes run very slow,
# low-CPU tools in the foreground.
# WAKE_STALL_TIMEOUT=300

# Optional: the same reaping for an INTERACTIVE turn — desk or phone — at a
# desk-appropriate patience, because somebody asked out loud and is standing
# there waiting (default: 120). Same two life signs as a wake.
# TURN_STALL_TIMEOUT=120

# Optional: CPU priority for background hands — wake units, detached builder
# jobs, and the out-of-band judges (defaults: 25 and 10). A wake or a build
# never competes at par for the processor with a turn somebody is waiting on;
# on an idle machine background work still gets everything. Priority is the
# only thing lowered — nothing pauses or kills background work for a turn.
# Empty disables either flag. specs/wake-queue.md rule 12a, jobs.md rule 2a.
# BACKGROUND_CPU_WEIGHT=25
# BACKGROUND_NICE=10

# Optional: a ceiling on a turn's WHOLE account walk in seconds (default: 900,
# 0 = none). The stall watchdog cannot catch a session that keeps emitting
# output forever, and a walk over the accounts runs one whole CLI session per
# account offered — so this bounds the walk, not any single run.
# TURN_CHAIN_TIMEOUT=900

# Optional: concurrent-turn context window (seconds, default 300, 0 = off).
# A wake that fires while an interactive turn (desk or phone) is in flight —
# or within this window of one finishing — is shown that exchange in its
# prompt: the user's message, and the reply once there is one. The wake is
# asked to behave like someone who just heard the answer given: regroup —
# carry its own point forward from there as ONE reply, never restating — and
# stay quiet only if it has nothing to add. Nothing is muted; it decides.
# WAKE_TURN_CONTEXT_WINDOW=300

# Optional: regroup window (seconds, default 180, 0 = off). When one session
# is speaking and another starts, the second is shown the first reply
# VERBATIM and asked to fold both things into ONE reply rather than queue its
# own thought away or fall silent. This is how long a published utterance
# counts as "being said right now" (a dead speaker's record is ignored
# immediately, whatever this is set to).
# LIVE_SPEECH_WINDOW=180

# Optional: wake coalescing window (seconds, default 900, 0 = off). A
# `crab wake-at` booking of scheduled kind whose fire time is within this
# window of a pending booking with the same reason is not added — the pending
# promise already covers it. Stops overlapping sessions from stacking near-
# duplicate timers that each re-announce the same progress.
# WAKE_COALESCE_WINDOW=900

# Optional: the ceiling on a self-scheduled wake session, seconds (default
# 7200). Every transient wake unit is booked with it as TimeoutStartSec — the
# absolute backstop for a runaway session, since the in-process stall watchdog
# cannot by construction reap one that keeps emitting output. Before this,
# only the standing random-interval timer had any ceiling at all.
# WAKE_RUNTIME_MAX=7200

# Optional: where the queue's own change ledger lives, and how many lines of it
# are kept. Every booking, cancellation, restoration, collapse and purge is
# written here, and the state block reads it to say what changed while she was
# away — a bulk restore means a cancellation was undone or the machine
# rebooted, and that used to go to /dev/null.
# WAKE_LEDGER="$HOME/.local/share/deskcrab/wakes/ledger.log"
# WAKE_LEDGER_KEEP=2000

# Optional: detached background jobs — `crab job "<description>"` runs work as
# its own headless claude session under a transient systemd user unit
# (deskcrab-job-<id>), so it survives the turn that launched it and never
# holds the conversation open. Jobs are silent: no TTS, no windows; output
# goes to JOBS_DIR/<id>.log with a JSON status sidecar, listed by `crab jobs`.
# Jobs run unattended builds nobody is waiting on, so they default to the
# strongest model at high effort.
# JOBS_DIR="$HOME/.local/share/deskcrab/jobs"
# JOB_MODEL="fable"
# JOB_EFFORT="high"
# JOBS_SHOW_FINISHED=6     # finished jobs shown by `crab jobs` and in the prompt
# JOBS_KEEP_DAYS=14        # prune finished job records after this many days

# Optional: long-term vector memory (`crab memory`, lib/memory.py). Records —
# directives (the user's standing rules) and notes (the assistant's own) —
# live in a sqlite-vec store under ~/.local/share/deskcrab/memory/, embedded
# with nomic-embed-text on a local ollama daemon. MEMORY_STORE=1 retrieves a
# "What I remember" block into every prompt build (wakes query by their
# reason, otherwise the wants shelf + conversation tail). Retrieval is
# fail-safe: an empty store adds nothing, and an unreachable embedder degrades
# to pinned records with a warning instead of breaking the prompt.
# MEMORY_STORE=1
# MEMORY_EMBED_MODEL="nomic-embed-text"
# MEMORY_EMBED_URL="http://localhost:11434/api/embed"
# The nightly ingest is two-stage (specs/memory-recall.md rule 27): the
# summariser condenses each window of the day, and the NIGHT JUDGE decides
# what earns a record. Every sleep judgment is the judge's — the summariser
# tier never decides what survives, and no cheaper model ever judges in the
# judge's place (specs/nightly.md rules 14c-14e).
# MEMORY_INGEST_MODEL="gpt-5.6-sol"  # the night judge: what earns a record
# MEMORY_INGEST_EFFORT="high"        # the judge's reasoning effort
# MEMORY_SUMMARY_MODEL="sonnet"      # stage 1: summaries only, never judgment
# MEMORY_SUMMARY_EFFORT="low"        # binds on a codex-named summariser only
# Reinforcement: after each turn's reply is delivered, a detached judge asks
# which of the injected records GENUINELY influenced the reply and reinforces
# only those (bumping last_used_at/use_count, which score retrieval). Records
# surfaced but ignored get no boost — mere retrieval never reinforces.
# MEMORY_JUDGE=1                 # set 0 to disable the turn-end judge
# MEMORY_JUDGE_MODEL="opus"      # judging use is taste — same tier as the sift

# Optional: the promise checker (lib/promise-check, specs/turn-pipeline.md
# rules 32a-32d). After a reply is delivered on any channel, a cheap pattern
# pre-check looks for the shape of a first-person commitment ("I'm wiring it
# now", "I've written it to the log"); only a reply that has one is judged by
# a small model against the tool calls the turn's own stream log records. An
# unkept commitment lands on the promise ledger and books an event wake
# minutes out at effort low, quoting the promise, so the work actually
# happens. The nightly sleep pass sweeps the whole day behind it.
# PROMISE_CHECK=1                   # set 0 to disable the checker entirely
# PROMISE_CHECK_MODEL="sonnet"      # the verifier — evidence-reading, not taste
# PROMISE_CHECK_FALLBACK_MODEL="haiku"  # when the verifier answers unparseably
# PROMISE_CHECK_WAKE_DELAY="3m"     # how far out the do-it-now wake fires
# PROMISE_CHECK_WAKE_CAP=3          # pending unkept-commitment wakes, at most
# PROMISE_CHECK_REBOOK_MAX=2        # wakes one verbatim promise may earn a day
# PROMISE_SWEEP_WAKE_AT="09:45"     # when the night sweep's misses are surfaced

# The chore gate (specs/turn-pipeline.md rule 16c): a displayed reply that
# hands the user a command line to run has that chore converted into ONE
# detached job — through the job door's own policy — and the display names
# the job instead. Fails open; benign command mentions are never touched.
# CHORE_GATE=1                      # set 0 to deliver displayed chores as written

# The night judge's other roles (specs/nightly.md rules 14c-14e): every sleep
# judgment — retention, deduplication, reconciliation, work selection — runs
# on the night judge, gpt-5.6-sol at high by default, with NO fallback model.
# The nightly ingest judge pair is MEMORY_INGEST_MODEL/_EFFORT above.
# PROMISE_SWEEP_MODEL="gpt-5.6-sol"  # the night sweep's judge — never the
#                                    # live checker's sonnet/haiku pair
# PROMISE_SWEEP_EFFORT="high"
# ENG_MERGE_MODEL="gpt-5.6-sol"      # the twin-merge judge (rule 53c)
# ENG_MERGE_EFFORT="high"
# NIGHT_WORK_MODEL="gpt-5.6-sol"     # the night-work selector (rule 58)
# NIGHT_WORK_EFFORT="high"
# NIGHT_WORK_CUTOFF="06:00"          # the hard dispatch line (rule 56)
# NIGHT_WORK_CAP=2                   # concurrent builders, the user's ceiling

# Optional: self-change awareness (lib/notice-selfchange, driven by
# systemd/deskcrab-notice-selfchange.path). Wakes the assistant when files
# that constitute her — wants, conduct, engineering, journal, memory, config,
# the deskcrab repo — change by a hand that was not hers. Enable with:
#   systemctl --user enable --now deskcrab-notice-selfchange.path
# Her own writers declare themselves via `crab touching <paths>` (wired into
# turns, wakes, jobs, the journal, and the memory store automatically).
# TOUCH_WINDOW=900            # seconds a `crab touching` declaration lasts
# NOTICE_SELF_SETTLE=120      # seconds a burst settles before ONE wake fires
# NOTICE_SELF_DEFERRALS=15    # live-session pushbacks before judging anyway
# NOTICE_SELF_RECENT=600      # "a session ran recently", for memory.db churn
# Personal directories to watch beyond the standard set (colon-separated).
# Give each one a PathChanged= line in a local drop-in of the .path unit too:
#   systemctl --user edit deskcrab-notice-selfchange.path
# SELF_WATCH_EXTRA="$HOME/SomewhereElse/Library"

# Optional: assistant persona name (default: Crab)
# The name the assistant sees in its system prompt: "You are <name>, a desktop
# voice assistant...". Set this to rename the assistant (e.g. "Beatrice",
# "Jarvis", etc). Also used as the notification/display title unless
# NOTIFY_NAME is set separately.
# ASSISTANT_NAME="Beatrice"

# Optional: assistant display name (default: ASSISTANT_NAME if set, else DeskCrab)
# Used as the notification title AND the title bar of the markdown display window.
# NOTIFY_NAME="DeskCrab"

# Optional: remote client (crab serve) — a phone talks to this machine.
# The assistant itself never moves: the phone is a microphone, a speaker, and a
# screen; the claude CLI, the prompt, the tools, and the conversation all stay
# here. Serving is off until SERVE_SECRET is set.
#
# Generate a secret with:  head -c 18 /dev/urandom | base64 | tr -d '/+='
# SERVE_SECRET="change-me"
#
# The listener binds loopback by default. Publishing it to a phone is a
# separate, deliberate act — a browser will not grant microphone access over
# plain HTTP, so put it behind TLS, e.g. Tailscale:
#   tailscale serve --bg https / http://127.0.0.1:8723
# SERVE_PORT=8723
# SERVE_BIND="127.0.0.1"
#
# Optional: seconds a single remote turn may take before it is abandoned.
# SERVE_TIMEOUT=600
#
# Optional: phone streaming voice granularity. 0 (the default) synthesises one
# opus clip per completed reply block — first audio waits for the whole block,
# which on a long turn can be most of a minute. 1 chunks the stream's text
# deltas into sentences with the same chunker the desk voice uses and ships a
# clip per sentence, so the phone starts hearing the reply while it is still
# being written. Off by default while it beds in.
# PHONE_SENTENCE_STREAM=1
#
# Optional: where the user is. Each phone message may carry the phone's own
# geolocation fix (the browser asks its permission once); a fresh fix on the
# post that creates a turn puts one line — "he is near <place>" — into that
# turn's context, and anything short of a fresh, well-formed fix puts nothing,
# silently. GEOCODE_URL is the reverse geocoder ({lat}/{lon} are filled in;
# set it empty to skip the lookup and carry plain coordinates); GEO_STALE_S
# is how old a fix may be and still count as "where he is"; the lookup is
# abandoned after GEO_LOOKUP_TIMEOUT_S so a slow geocoder can never hold a
# turn.
# GEOCODE_URL="https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/reverse?format=jsonv2&lat={lat}&lon={lon}&zoom=17"
# GEO_STALE_S=600
# GEO_LOOKUP_TIMEOUT_S=2

# Optional: Web Push (crab notify) — reach the phone even with the page closed.
# Subscribe from the phone page's bell button; send with `crab notify <text>`.
# Needs the python-cryptography package (a stock system package on most
# distros); VAPID keys and subscriptions live under
# ~/.local/share/deskcrab/webpush/. PUSH_SUBJECT is the RFC 8292 `sub` claim —
# how the push service's operators could reach the sender (mailto: or https:
# URL). The default identifies this project on GitHub.
# PUSH_SUBJECT="mailto:you@example.com"
